# width: 120
# height: 40
# kbd (keyboard-purist VS Code user): VS Code's Settings screen opens
# with the caret in "Search settings", so the reflex on Ctrl+, is to
# type the name of the row you came for. mnml's overlay opened with the
# LIST focused and every bare letter live, so `quit` — the obvious
# query for the `Confirm on quit` row — was a small program: `q` saved
# and closed the box with no toast, and `uit` fell into the buffer
# underneath, which four keystrokes aimed at a search box had now
# dirtied (hunt/findings-2026-09-21/kbd-settings-typeahead.md).
#
# The standard profile's box is type-to-filter now. The vim profile
# keeps `h j k l r R q g G [ ]` — that is the family convention and
# what a vim user's hands expect — and both profiles ask before `R`
# throws every setting away.
write a.txt "alpha\n"
open a.txt
expect dirty false

command view.settings
expect screen contains "▸ Line numbers:"
# The footer advertises what actually works here: no letter commands.
# (At 120 columns the footer is down to its tightest form, which is why
# it is `ctrl+r reset` rather than `type to search` that is asserted —
# `hintFor`'s five standard forms have their own unit test.)
expect screen contains "ctrl+r reset"
expect screen lacks "r/R reset"

type "quit"
# The box is still open, with a query in it…
expect screen contains "UI · Editor · AI · Integrations · Reset"
expect screen contains "󰍉 quit"
expect screen contains "Confirm on quit:"
expect screen lacks "▸ Line numbers:"
# …and nothing reached the file.
expect dirty false
expect screen lacks "uitalpha"
key esc
key esc

# Every letter that used to be a command is a query character now —
# `h` and `l` no longer adjust the focused row behind the search.
command view.settings
expect screen contains "off / [on]"
type "hjklr"
expect screen contains "󰍉 hjklr"
expect screen contains "no setting matches"
key esc
expect screen contains "▸ Line numbers:"
expect screen contains "off / [on]"

# Ctrl+R is the reset the footer names, and it is a chord rather than a
# letter, so it survives in a box you can type into.
key right
expect screen contains "[off] / on"
key ctrl+r
expect screen contains "off / [on]"

# `R` resets nothing here — it is a query byte.
type "R"
expect screen contains "󰍉 R"
key esc
key esc

# Reset-all is the Reset section's action row, and it asks first with
# Cancel focused, so Enter on reflex changes nothing.
command view.settings
key right
expect screen contains "[off] / on"
key end
expect screen contains "▸ Reset all to defaults"
key enter
expect screen contains "Reset settings"
expect screen contains "Reset every setting to its default?"
expect screen contains "[C]ancel"
key enter
expect screen lacks "Reset every setting to its default?"
expect screen contains "▸ Reset all to defaults"
# Esc answers no too.
key enter
expect screen contains "Reset every setting to its default?"
key esc
expect screen lacks "Reset every setting to its default?"
# …and the Reset choice is the one that does it.
key enter
key r
expect screen lacks "Reset every setting to its default?"
key home
expect screen contains "▸ Line numbers:"
expect screen contains "off / [on]"
key esc

# The vim profile is untouched: bare letters are its commands, and `R`
# raises the same ask.
command editor.use_vim
command view.settings
expect screen contains "r/R reset"
expect screen lacks "ctrl+r reset"
expect screen contains "▸ Line numbers:"
key l
expect screen contains "[off] / on"
key r
expect screen contains "off / [on]"
key R
expect screen contains "Reset every setting to its default?"
key esc
expect screen lacks "Reset every setting to its default?"
# `q` still saves and closes in vim, where it is documented and expected.
key q
expect screen lacks "UI · Editor · AI · Integrations · Reset"
expect dirty false
command editor.use_standard
